<section id="study" style={{ padding: "2rem 1rem 2.5rem" }}>
  <div class="container" style={{ maxWidth: "900px" }}>
    <span class="section-title">Before you book</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading">How to prepare so you don't waste an attempt</h2>
    <p style={{ color: "var(--muted)", fontSize: "0.95rem", lineHeight: 1.75, margin: "0.75rem 0 1rem" }}>
      These are practical, time-boxed exams. Video courses alone are not enough: you need hands-on repetition with kubectl, YAML, cluster troubleshooting and the official documentation. Most candidates need 6–10 weeks for CKA, then 4–8 weeks for CKS. CNPE is a broader platform exam; plan 8–12 weeks if you already have platform experience.
    </p>
    <ul style={{ margin: 0, paddingLeft: "1.2rem", color: "var(--muted)", fontSize: "0.92rem", lineHeight: 1.85 }}>
      <li><strong style={{ color: "var(--text)" }}>Use the current curriculum.</strong> CKA and CKS are based on Kubernetes v1.37 at this check. Study the current domains and weightings, not an older course outline.</li>
      <li><strong style={{ color: "var(--text)" }}>Practice under time pressure.</strong> The CKA is two hours and performance-based. Speed with kubectl, imperative commands and YAML editing is part of the exam.</li>
      <li><strong style={{ color: "var(--text)" }}>Use the Killer.sh simulator.</strong> Linux Foundation registration includes two simulator attempts with 36 hours of access each. Treat the first as a diagnostic and the second as a timed rehearsal.</li>
      <li><strong style={{ color: "var(--text)" }}>Do not book before two clean practice runs.</strong> A voucher is a prepaid attempt, not a diagnostic. If you are scoring below 75–80% on timed practice, keep studying.</li>
      <li><strong style={{ color: "var(--text)" }}>For CKS, pass CKA first.</strong> Then focus on cluster hardening, supply chain security, runtime security and policy. The CKS exam assumes you already administer clusters fluently.</li>
      <li><strong style={{ color: "var(--text)" }}>For CNPE, build something real.</strong> Practice GitOps with Argo CD or Flux, CRDs and operators, observability with Prometheus and Grafana, and policy with admission controllers. The exam is about platform design, not isolated commands.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style={{ color: "var(--muted)", fontSize: "0.9rem", lineHeight: 1.75, margin: "1rem 0 0" }}>
      Browse prep courses by topic: <a href="/topics/kubernetes" style={{ color: "var(--brand)", fontWeight: 600 }}>Kubernetes</a>,
      <a href="/topics/devops" style={{ color: "var(--brand)", fontWeight: 600 }}>DevOps</a>,
      <a href="/topics/linux" style={{ color: "var(--brand)", fontWeight: 600 }}>Linux</a> and
      <a href="/topics/it-certifications" style={{ color: "var(--brand)", fontWeight: 600 }}>IT certifications</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

